> (It would certainly make sense to remove a user interface inconsistency.)

You would have to be very careful to meet your new requirement while
preserving backward compatibility. First there was no automatic pickup
at all. Then autopickup was introduced for all objects (including
cockatrice corpses). At latest when the weight system was implemented
the need for pickup_types evolved. For the sake of compatibility a
missing and thus empty pickup_types since then meant picking up all
types.

All solutions as well satisfying autopickup exceptions as well I could
think of are ugly.

> And a patch might even be trivial; I haven't inspected the code thoroughly,
> but it seems that it *might* suffice to remove 'compat' from the if-clause
> in options.c (line 1659):
> 
>                     if (compat || negated || initial) {
>                         /* for backwards compatibility, "pickup" without a
>                            value is a synonym for autopickup of all types
>                            (and during initialization, we can't prompt yet) */
>                         flags.pickup = !negated;
>                         return;
> 
> (Folks more familiar with the source code may confirm that or correct me.)

Though not being familiar with the nethack source I think I can assure
you that dropping compat there would not change anything =:). The value
of compat is only true if the option string begins with at least the
first eight characters of "pickup_types" and is yet at most six
characters long.

May be the time line I gave above was not correct as we now might have
had another pickup option, that apparently had a hybrid use as a boolean
or a list ... until it was somehow annihilated.
Hey, that would have broken compatibility!


